# 21. A vault of documents

The shelf of [[06-sql-like-queries|chapter 6]] was a list written by hand. Here the
same books are documents in a folder, and HQL reads the folder. This part of
the book is about knowledge: documents, the links between them, their
meaning, and the agents that read them.

## A vault is a folder

A **vault** is a directory of Markdown and HyperMarkDown documents, marked as
one by an `.hmd/` directory at its root. The reading-club vault looks like this:

```text
examples/reading-club/
├── authors/
│   ├── frank-herbert.hmd
│   ├── jrr-tolkien.hmd
│   ├── mary-shelley.hmd
│   ├── virginia-woolf.hmd
│   └── walt-whitman.hmd
├── books/
│   ├── a-room-of-ones-own.hmd
│   ├── dune.hmd
│   ├── frankenstein.hmd
│   ├── leaves-of-grass.hmd
│   ├── mrs-dalloway.hmd
│   ├── the-common-reader.hmd
│   └── the-hobbit.hmd
├── club.hmd
├── frankenstein-inspires-dune.hmd
├── librarian.hmd
├── .hmd/config.toml
└── .hql/config.toml
```

`.hmd/` makes the directory a vault. `.hql/config.toml` makes it an HQL
project too, which configures the programs run in it; the two are separate,
and a vault need not be a project
([[13-working-with-hql#projects-and-vaults|chapter 13]]).

The vault's `.hmd/config.toml` says where its cards start, with the key
`location_path`: a path against the vault root, `.`, the vault root itself,
when the file does not set it. The reading club keeps its cards at the vault
root, and says so anyway:

```toml
location_path = "."
```

The key used to be called `wiki`, with the default `doc/wiki`. A config that
still sets `wiki` is refused with a message naming `location_path`, and the
vault is not read until the key is renamed.

That directory is the **namespace root**. A document is a card exactly when
it lies below it, and a card's path is written from it, so `books/dune.hmd`
above. A file of the vault outside it, a `README.md` beside `.hmd/` in a
vault whose `location_path` is `doc/wiki`, is not a card. A card is a `.hmd` file, or a
`.md` or `.markdown` file whose body is plain Markdown.

Each document is a **card**. A card has a header of metadata and a body
someone wrote:

```markdown
---
title: Dune
metadata:
  category: book
  author: Frank Herbert
  year: 1965
  genre: novel
  pages: 412
  status: reading
---
# Dune

A novel by [[frank-herbert]]. A desert planet, a spice, and a family that inherits both.
```

A program run inside a vault reads it: `hql` takes the nearest directory at
or above the program's file that holds `.hmd/`, or at or above the working
directory for an expression or standard input. `--vault <directory>` names
one instead, and `--bare` runs with none. The cards are read through the
builtin package `hmd`, which the reading-club's project imports for every
program ("The vault itself" below):

```sh
hql --vault examples/reading-club eval 'cards | size'
```

```hql
cards | size
```

```text
15
```

## `cards`

`cards` is every card of the vault, as a sequence. Its order is the order a
sorted walk of the folder reaches each document — the entries of each
directory by name, depth first — so a program answers the same on every
machine:

```hql
cards | take(4) | map(c => c.name)
```

```text
["frank-herbert", "jrr-tolkien", "mary-shelley", "virginia-woolf"]
```

A card has the fields of a document: its `name` (the file stem), `title`,
`path`, `format`, `body`, its `kind`, and `metadata`, the header's tree.

## A link is an optional card

`@[[name]]` refers to a card by name: `@` followed by the link a document
writes, `[[name]]`. A link
may point at a document nobody has written yet, so its type is `Option<Card>`,
and reading a field through it reads through the option:

```hql
@[[dune]].title
```

```text
"Dune"
```

```hql
@[[nowhere]].title
```

```text
none
```

## Metadata is Data

A card's metadata is the `Data` of [[data|chapter 2]], so every question
from chapters 2 and 6 applies. The books are the cards whose category says so:

```hql
cards
| filter(c => c.metadata.category == "book")
| map(c => c.title)
```

```text
["A Room of One's Own", "Dune", "Frankenstein", "Leaves of Grass", "Mrs Dalloway", "The Common Reader", "The Hobbit"]
```

## The reading list

The club's reading list is the books still to read, by title:

```hql
cards
| filter(c => c.metadata.status == "todo")
| sort(by = c => c.title)
| map(c => c.title)
```

```text
["Leaves of Grass", "The Common Reader", "The Hobbit"]
```

Read it as three steps, each consuming the one before: `filter` decides each
card on its own, `sort` needs every card before it can place the first, and
`map` turns each card into its title. Nothing is stored in between, and
nothing is a loop.

No one maintains this list. Mark a book `status: done` in its header and the
next run of the query no longer lists it.

## Writing cards

The formats of [[08-output|chapter 8]] apply to a card as to any value. A
card written as Markdown is its body; a list of cards, a list of links:

```hql
cards | filter(c => c.metadata.category == "author") | markdown
```

```text
- [[frank-herbert]]
- [[jrr-tolkien]]
- [[mary-shelley]]
- [[virginia-woolf]]
- [[walt-whitman]]
```

## Grouping cards

`group` partitions the books by a header value, as it partitioned the shelf:

```hql
cards
| filter(c => c.metadata.category == "book")
| group(by = c => c.metadata.status)
| map(g => {status: g.key, books: g.items | map(c => c.title)})
```

```text
{{books: ["A Room of One's Own", "Frankenstein"], status: "done"}, {books: ["Dune", "Mrs Dalloway"], status: "reading"}, {books: ["Leaves of Grass", "The Common Reader", "The Hobbit"], status: "todo"}}
```

A typed view of a card works as the views of chapter 6 did, and the pages per
author are a map over each group and a sum — map, then reduce:

```hql
fn pages(c: Card) : Int = match c.metadata.pages { some(Int n) => n, _ => 0 }

cards
| filter(c => c.metadata.category == "book")
| group(by = c => c.metadata.author)
| map(g => {author: g.key, pages: g.items | map(pages) | sum})
```

```text
{{author: "Virginia Woolf", pages: 610}, {author: "Frank Herbert", pages: 412}, {author: "Mary Shelley", pages: 280}, {author: "Walt Whitman", pages: 145}, {author: "J. R. R. Tolkien", pages: 310}}
```

## Kinds of card

A card whose header declares a relation between two other cards is a
`RelationCard`; every other card is a `ConceptCard`. `of_type` keeps the cards
of one kind:

```hql
cards | of_type(RelationCard) | map(c => c.title)
```

```text
["Frankenstein inspires Dune"]
```

[[22-graphs|Chapter 22]] is about what that relation means: cards and the
links between them form a graph.

## The vault itself

`cards` is a binding of the builtin package `hmd`, `cards = vault.cards`, over
`vault`, the value the host seeds when it is given a vault:

```hql
[vault.name, vault.cards | size | text]
```

```text
["reading-club", 15]
```

A vault is HyperMarkdown's, and so are `vault` and `cards`. They are bound
while the package `hmd` is **active**, and a run that has a vault activates
it: the vault found at or above the program, or the one `--vault` names. A
program in a vault writes `cards` with no import.

A run with no vault activates `hmd` by importing it: `import hmd` in the
program, `--import hmd` on the command line, or `[modules] import` in the
project's `.hql/config.toml`:

```toml
[modules]
import = ["hmd"]
```

With no vault and no import, `cards` names what is missing:

<!-- run: novault -->
```hql
cards | size
```

```text
check error[unknown-name]: `cards` is HyperMarkdown's: write `import hmd`, or run with `--import hmd`, to bind it
 --> <expression>:1:1
  |
1 | cards | size
  | ^^^^^
```

With `hmd` imported and no vault, `cards` has nothing to read, and the
check says so:

<!-- run: novault -->
```hql
import hmd
cards | size
```

```text
check error[unknown-name]: `cards` needs a vault: run inside one or pass --vault <directory>
 --> <expression>:2:1
  |
2 | cards | size
  | ^^^^^
```

## A vault is a namespace

A card is a file with a place in a vault, and the place is what makes it
addressable by name: `[[dune]]` names a card because there is one tree to
resolve the name in. That tree is a **namespace**, rooted at the vault's
namespace root, and a vault is exactly one namespace. `cards` holds what a
wikilink can reach and nothing else, and a card's `path` is its place in the
vault, relative to the namespace root, so the cards of one folder are a filter
on the path:

```hql
cards | filter(c => c.path | starts_with("books/")) | size
```

```text
7
```

HQL resolves a reference exactly as HyperMarkdown does, through
HyperMarkdown's own resolver, so `hql` and `hmd lint` always name the same
card. A `[[name]]` in a card is looked up from that card: a path that opens
with `/`, `./` or `../` is followed; otherwise a name the card imports, then
the card's own folder and each folder above it, nearest first, then the
folders it imports with `*`, and last a search of the whole vault. A search
that finds two cards chooses neither, and the reference has to be
qualified, `[[books/dune]]`. `@[[dune]]` in a program is resolved as though
written at the namespace root. Where `dune.hmd` and `dune.md` sit side by
side, `[[dune]]` is the `.hmd` card and `[[dune.md]]` the other, whose name
keeps its extension.

## Other vaults

A second vault is a second namespace. It is reached as a configured endpoint of
kind `vault` ([[09-io/configured|endpoints by id]]), with its own
`cards` and `graph`, and its cards are never merged into `cards`: a wikilink
in one vault never resolves into another, and a relation between cards of two
vaults is a join's result, not a link. The field station of the data sources
examples configures two:

<!-- run: --vault examples/endpoints/station -->
```hql
journal = source("journal")
archive = source("archive")
[journal.cards | map(c => c.name), archive.cards | map(c => c.name)]
```

```text
[["kite", "owl", "wren"], ["finch", "heron"]]
```

## What is next

A document that writes `[[frank-herbert]]` links to another.
[[22-graphs|Chapter 22]] follows the links, and the cards and links together
are a graph.
